// ============================================================================
// Hunt: ARTEX agentic AI framework - FBI FLASH-20261113-001 IOCs and
//   exploitation behaviour (FBI, November 13, 2026)
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1190 (Exploit Public-Facing Application), T1505.003
//   (Server Software Component: Web Shell), T1078 (Valid Accounts),
//   T1606.002 (Forge Web Credentials: SAML Tokens), T1090 (Proxy),
//   T1572 (Protocol Tunneling), T1071.001 (Application Layer Protocol:
//   Web Protocols), T1041 (Exfiltration Over C2 Channel)
// Data: Microsoft Defender XDR - DeviceNetworkEvents, DeviceFileEvents,
//   DeviceProcessEvents, AADSignInEventsBeta
// Write-up: https://amitvijayan.com/journal/articles/daily-cyber-threat-brief-november-16-2026.html
// Logic: ARTEX is an open-source, LLM-driven multi-agent penetration
//   testing framework written in Go (released July 2026 by "Autumn-27",
//   repo removed October 8, 2026; forks and a commercial ARTEX PRO keep
//   it available). The FBI's FLASH-20261113-001 (TLP:CLEAR, November 13,
//   2026) says multiple threat actors have weaponized it since at least
//   August 2026, with some intrusions going from initial targeting to
//   privileged access in under three hours. Notable campaigns: financially
//   motivated attacks on South Korean financial institutions (personal
//   data of ~67,000 people exfiltrated) and the ConnectWise Cloud Backup
//   supply-chain compromise reaching 2,250+ downstream tenants. Censys
//   counted 1,600+ publicly exposed ARTEX consoles as of early November.
//   This file hunts the FLASH's published IOCs and behaviours. It does
//   NOT duplicate vapor-panda-artex-espionage.kql (CrowdStrike's Vapor
//   Panda campaign: password spraying, forged tokens, bulk mailbox reads
//   - no IOCs were published for that campaign) or
//   connectwise-service-principal-hijack.kql (the SkyKick/ConnectWise
//   service-principal pattern itself). The FBI warns these IPs are
//   historically observed infrastructure in the activity window and may
//   since have been reassigned - corroborate any hit with current
//   telemetry before blocking. Any hit on Variant 1 or 2 deserves a
//   look the same day: ARTEX compresses the intrusion window to hours.
// ============================================================================

// -- Variant 1: FBI FLASH IP IOCs - any connection or sign-in from the
//    published ARTEX infrastructure --
//    24 IPs, grouped by context in the FLASH: 38.244.50.120 is the ARTEX
//    host / open directory (South Korea financial campaign); the
//    144.172.64.0/24 and 187.15.88-90.0/24 addresses are the ConnectWise
//    campaign (sign-ins and Graph API activity in customer environments,
//    transient VPN IPs relevant October 15-16, 2026); the 101.53.80.20
//    block is proxy IPs for the South Korea campaign; 199.19.95.66-68 are
//    WISP C2 nodes and 91.92.240.238, 85.11.168.45, 178.16.54.129,
//    31.77.229.35 are ARTEX servers (Storm-3358 campaign, active as of
//    November 6, 2026); 85.11.167.52 is a Storm-3358 C2 server. The
//    30-day window covers the FLASH activity window (since August 2026
//    activity, review from at least October 1 per the FBI).
let FlashIPs = dynamic([
    "38.244.50.120",
    "144.172.64.60", "144.172.64.99", "144.172.64.128",
    "187.15.88.186", "187.15.90.217", "187.15.89.186",
    "101.53.80.20", "205.214.59.31", "124.155.252.63", "154.201.79.246",
    "23.248.249.90", "23.158.220.98", "103.248.148.84", "203.160.133.172",
    "209.209.85.38",
    "199.19.95.66", "199.19.95.67", "199.19.95.68",
    "91.92.240.238", "85.11.168.45", "178.16.54.129", "31.77.229.35",
    "85.11.167.52"
]);
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(30d)
| where RemoteIP in (FlashIPs)
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, RemoteIP, RemoteUrl, RemotePort,
    LocalIP, InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine,
    ActionType
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 1b: the same IOCs in Entra sign-ins --
//    The ConnectWise campaign signed into customer tenants and hit Graph
//    API from these addresses. A sign-in from a FLASH IP is a stronger
//    signal than a bare network connection - check it even if the
//    network variant is quiet (proxies and VPN egress hide the source).
AADSignInEventsBeta
| where Timestamp > ago(30d)
| where IPAddress in (FlashIPs)
| project Timestamp, AccountUpn, AppDisplayName, IPAddress, Country,
    UserAgent, ErrorCode, DeviceDetail
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 2: LLM API egress and the xcai.pro reseller --
//    ARTEX has no model of its own: it calls external LLM providers
//    (Anthropic Claude, OpenAI GPT, DeepSeek, GLM, Grok, Kimi). The
//    FLASH names api.anthropic.com, api.deepseek.com, open.bigmodel.cn
//    and api.x.ai as observed outbound API calls and xcai.pro as the LLM
//    API reseller used in the South Korea financial campaign. The FBI's
//    own guidance is to investigate unexpected calls alongside other
//    suspicious activity - a sanctioned AI tool calling these hosts is
//    normal, a server process or an unknown binary doing so is not.
//    xcai.pro has no legitimate business in most estates: treat any
//    contact with it as a hit. Summarize by device and process so one
//    noisy workstation does not bury a quiet server.
let LLMHosts = dynamic([
    "xcai.pro", "api.deepseek.com", "open.bigmodel.cn", "api.x.ai",
    "api.anthropic.com"
]);
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(14d)
| where RemoteUrl has_any (LLMHosts)
| where InitiatingProcessFileName !in~ (
    "chrome.exe", "msedge.exe", "firefox.exe", "brave.exe",
    "opera.exe", "iexplore.exe"
)
| summarize Connections = count(),
            Hosts = make_set(RemoteUrl, 10),
            FirstSeen = min(Timestamp),
            LastSeen = max(Timestamp)
    by DeviceName, InitiatingProcessFileName
| order by Connections desc

// -- Variant 3: ARTEX web shells and webhook.site exfiltration --
//    The FLASH lists *.aspx and *.jsp web shells in unexpected web
//    application paths (agents deploy JSP and PHP shells, including via
//    MySQL log poisoning) and command output sent to rotating
//    webhook.site endpoints, plus outbound transfers of structured data
//    (names, phone numbers, financial information) over HTTPS. Flag
//    newly created .aspx/.jsp files under web roots and any webhook.site
//    contact: triage the shell hits against the application's own
//    deployments - a file your developers did not put there is the
//    compromise signal.
DeviceFileEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where ActionType == "FileCreated"
| where FileName endswith ".aspx" or FileName endswith ".jsp"
| where FolderPath has_any ("inetpub", "wwwroot", "webapps", "htdocs",
    "tomcat", "webroot")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, FolderPath, SHA256,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by TimeGenerated desc

// -- Variant 3b: webhook.site exfiltration channel --
DeviceNetworkEvents
| where Timestamp > ago(14d)
| where RemoteUrl contains "webhook.site"
| project Timestamp, DeviceName, RemoteUrl, RemoteIP,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine
| order by Timestamp desc

// -- Variant 4: ARTEX post-exploitation tooling on endpoints --
//    The FLASH's persistence/C2 inventory: open-source RATs WISP,
//    vshell and AdaptixC2, traffic tunneled via Chisel and SoftEther,
//    plus SSH key-pair creation and Elastic IP allocation. Hunt the
//    named tools by process name or command line. "wisp" and "vshell"
//    are short strings - expect some noise and triage each hit against
//    the host's role; a tunneling tool on a server that should never
//    run one is the signal.
DeviceProcessEvents
| where TimeGenerated > ago(30d)
| where FileName has_any ("chisel", "softether", "wisp", "vshell",
    "adaptix")
    or ProcessCommandLine has_any ("chisel", "softether", "webhook.site")
| project TimeGenerated, DeviceName, FileName, ProcessCommandLine,
    InitiatingProcessFileName, InitiatingProcessCommandLine, AccountName
| order by TimeGenerated desc
